{"tour":[{"chapter":"Welcome","page":"home","title":"Welcome! Here is what this app does","body":"Every business that advertises asks the same question: <b>which ads really bring in sales, and where should the next dollar go?</b> Google built a free tool called <b>Meridian</b> that answers it with statistics. This tour shows you the answers for a made-up company that advertises on 6 channels. You only click Next; there is nothing to type.","anchor":"home-hero"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"upload","title":"Upload your data","body":"Everything starts here. Upload a CSV or Excel file with one row per week: the date, your sales and what you spent on each channel. No file yet? Load the sample data, or open the demo results to look around first.","anchor":"s1-upload"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"step1","title":"Your data","body":"This is the file you uploaded, one row per week. It says how much the company spent on each channel (TV, Radio, Search\u2026) and how much it sold that week. In the app, <b>Preview the table</b> opens the rows.","anchor":"s1-data"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"step1","title":"Which column is the date, and which is sales","body":"Here you tell the app which column holds the week and which number you want to grow, such as sales. The small chart shows that number over time, so you can check it looks right before going on.","anchor":"s1-time"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"step1","title":"Your ad channels","body":"Each card is one place the company spends on ads. A card needs the column with that channel's spend and, if you have it, the column with its impressions or clicks. The app found all six on its own. You can rename a card, change its columns, add one or delete one.","anchor":"s1-channels"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"step1","title":"Other things that move sales","body":"Discounts, price changes or the season also push sales up and down. Pick those columns here, so the model doesn't give your ads credit for a sale that a discount caused.","anchor":"s1-controls"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"step1","title":"Run the model","body":"Once every column is set, choose how long Meridian should spend learning and press <b>Run model</b>. <b>Quick check</b> takes about 30 seconds and <b>Standard</b> about a minute.","anchor":"s1-run"},{"chapter":"Step 1 \u00b7 Data & model","page":"ready","title":"Model ready: how good is it?","body":"When the run finishes, this page grades the model. <b>0.97</b> means it explains 97% of the week-to-week ups and downs. <b>3.8%</b> is how far off it is in an average week. <b>Converged</b> means it finished learning properly.","anchor":"s1-ready"},{"chapter":"Step 2 \u00b7 Insights","page":"step2","title":"What each $1 brought back","body":"Each bar is a channel's <b>ROI</b>: the sales it brought back for every $1 spent. <b>Email</b> returned about <b>$10.62</b>; <b>Display</b> about <b>$1.61</b>. Bars to the right of the red dashed line paid for themselves. The thin line through each bar shows how sure the model is: the longer it is, the less sure.","anchor":"s2-roi"},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Real sales vs. the model's guess","body":"Two lines, one for real sales each week and one for the sales the model expected. The lowest line is the baseline: the sales you would get with no ads at all. If the real and expected lines stay close, the model has learned how your sales move. Big gaps in a few weeks usually mean something happened that the data doesn't show, like a stock-out.","anchor":"mr-expected_actual","chart":"expected_actual","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Fit scores","body":"Three numbers that grade the model. R-squared is the share of week-to-week change it explains. MAPE is how far off it is in an average week. wMAPE is the same, but big weeks count more. R-squared close to 1 is good. MAPE and wMAPE under 10% are good for weekly sales.","anchor":"mr-fit_table","chart":"fit_table","w":1446,"h":237},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"What made up total sales","body":"Each bar is one piece of your sales: the baseline (sales with no ads) and what each channel added, biggest first. The pieces add up to 100%. The longer a channel's bar, the more sales it brought in. It says nothing yet about cost; that comes in the ROI charts.","anchor":"mr-channel_drivers","chart":"channel_drivers","w":1446,"h":490},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Share of sales","body":"The whole circle is all your sales, split in two: the share your ads brought in (all channels together) and the baseline that would have happened anyway. The bigger the ads slice, the more your sales depend on marketing. A big baseline means many customers buy with or without ads.","anchor":"mr-contribution_pie","chart":"contribution_pie","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Share of money vs. share of sales","body":"For each channel, one bar is its share of the ad budget and the other is its share of the sales the ads brought in. The green mark is its ROI. When a channel's sales bar is longer than its money bar, it brings in more than its share of the budget. Those channels are cheap for what they do.","anchor":"mr-spend_outcome","chart":"spend_outcome","w":647,"h":731},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Sales from each channel, week by week","body":"Each colored band is the extra sales from one channel in each week. A band that gets thicker means that channel brought in more in those weeks, often because you spent more on it then.","anchor":"mr-contrib_by_time","chart":"contrib_by_time","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Which channel ranked first, over time","body":"Each line is one channel's rank by extra sales, from one period to the next. Rank 1 brought in the most. Lines that cross mean channels swapped places. A channel that stays at the top is your steady earner.","anchor":"mr-contrib_rank","chart":"contrib_rank","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Money back for every $1 (ROI)","body":"One bar per channel. The height is the sales each $1 of spend brought back. The thin line on top is the range the true value is likely in. Above 1 means the channel paid for itself. A long thin line means the model isn't sure, often because spend on that channel barely changed.","anchor":"mr-roi_channel","chart":"roi_channel","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Return vs. effect per ad","body":"Each bubble is a channel. Higher means more money back per $1. Further right means more sales for each ad people saw. Bigger bubbles had more spend. Bubbles up and to the right are working best. A big bubble low on the chart is a lot of money for a small return.","anchor":"mr-roi_effectiveness","chart":"roi_effectiveness","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Return now vs. return on the next $1","body":"Each bubble is a channel. Across is the average return so far. Up is the return on the next dollar you add. High bubbles can still take more money. Bubbles near the bottom are close to full: more money there buys little.","anchor":"mr-roi_marginal","chart":"roi_marginal","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"Cost to bring in $1 of extra sales","body":"ROI turned upside down: how much you pay, on each channel, to bring in one more dollar of sales. Shorter bars are cheaper. Under $1 means the channel earns back more than it costs.","anchor":"mr-cpik_channel","chart":"cpik_channel","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Meridian's own charts","page":"report","title":"What happens if you spend more or less","body":"One curve per channel: the sales it brings at different spend levels, from nothing up to double what you spend now. Steep parts mean more money still brings more sales. Where a curve flattens out, the channel is full and extra money mostly goes to waste.","anchor":"mr-response_curves","chart":"response_curves","w":2463,"h":1377},{"chapter":"Step 3 \u00b7 Budget plan","page":"step3","title":"A better budget","body":"In the app, <b>Optimize budget</b> keeps the same total and moves money from channels that are full to ones that still pay back, never changing a channel by more than \u00b130%. Blue bars mean spend more; red bars mean spend less.","anchor":"s3-settings"},{"chapter":"Budget plan charts","page":"report","title":"How the plan moves money","body":"One bar per channel: how much more or less money it gets in the plan. Bars above zero get more money, bars below give some up. The total budget stays the same.","anchor":"mr-spend_delta","chart":"spend_delta","w":1446,"h":624},{"chapter":"Budget plan charts","page":"report","title":"The planned budget split","body":"The whole circle is the planned budget. Each slice is one channel's share. Compare it with how you split money today to see the change at a glance.","anchor":"mr-spend_allocation","chart":"spend_allocation","w":1446,"h":474},{"chapter":"Budget plan charts","page":"report","title":"Extra sales from the plan","body":"The first bar is the sales your ads bring in with today's split. Each step after it is one channel's change under the plan, up or down. The last bar is the sales with the plan. Some channels lose a little and others gain more. The gap between the first and last bar is what the whole plan adds.","anchor":"mr-outcome_delta","chart":"outcome_delta","w":1446,"h":624},{"chapter":"Budget plan charts","page":"report","title":"The plan on each response curve","body":"The same curves as before, with marks for today's spend and for the planned spend. The plan moves money off the flat parts of curves and onto the steep parts.","anchor":"mr-optimized_curves","chart":"optimized_curves","w":2432,"h":1296},{"chapter":"GeoX (Lift test)","page":"geox","title":"New: GeoX (Lift test), a real experiment","body":"A model learns from the past, so it can be fooled. A <b>lift test</b> is an experiment: run ads in some regions, keep them off in similar ones, and measure the gap. In the app, you press <b>Pick test regions</b>, then <b>Run the test and measure</b>. GeoX has to find a number we hid in the data.","anchor":"gx-plan"},{"chapter":"Your turn","page":"step1","title":"You're ready for your own data","body":"Click <b>Change data</b>, then upload a spreadsheet with one row per week: a date, your sales and the spend on each channel. The app finds the columns for you. Stuck on a word? Open <b>Help &amp; glossary</b> in the sidebar.","anchor":"s1-data"}],"sample_rows":[{"week":"1/2/22","TV":878819,"Radio":143024,"Search":274926,"Email":85312,"Sales":6142072},{"week":"1/9/22","TV":728492,"Radio":147199,"Search":252129,"Email":86348,"Sales":6981694},{"week":"1/16/22","TV":526108,"Radio":163168,"Search":296951,"Email":98583,"Sales":7491688},{"week":"1/23/22","TV":809295,"Radio":96505,"Search":263929,"Email":81632,"Sales":8156830},{"week":"1/30/22","TV":595081,"Radio":128794,"Search":281549,"Email":101878,"Sales":7712426},{"week":"2/6/22","TV":881691,"Radio":242506,"Search":171502,"Email":90279,"Sales":8339120}],"summary":[{"channel":"TV","spend":125869216.1653,"pct_spend":40.9245,"impressions":2298552122.381,"incremental":327511975.8987,"incremental_lo":178616911.9454,"incremental_hi":485668960.8136,"pct_contribution":25.7095,"roi":2.602,"roi_lo":1.4191,"roi_hi":3.8585,"mroi":1.6278,"effectiveness":0.1425,"cpik":0.427,"is_total":false},{"channel":"Radio","spend":30854526.7605,"pct_spend":10.0319,"impressions":1253451338.198,"incremental":210211249.2888,"incremental_lo":73983289.2421,"incremental_hi":351128502.4389,"pct_contribution":16.5015,"roi":6.813,"roi_lo":2.3978,"roi_hi":11.3801,"mroi":3.9498,"effectiveness":0.1677,"cpik":0.18